Abstract
Herein, multi-core-shell structure Co/FeC $@$ N-doped hollow carbon (Co/FeC@NHC) with tunable carbon shell thickness is well crafted by a novel and simple strategy. Novel core–shell structure consisting of polydopamine (PDA) shell with different thickness and bimetal-based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) Co/Fe core with cubic morphology are first prepared, followed by a thermal and etching treatments to fabricate hollow composite materials composed of multiple Co/FeC cores evenly distributed in N-doped carbon shell. PDA acts as a carbon and nitrogen source simultaneously to form N-doped hollow carbon in this procedure. Creatively, the N-doped hollow carbon shell protects Co/FeC core and against the degradation, in addition to enhance the conductivity of Co/FeC $@$ NHC. Through adjusting the PDA shell thickness simply, Co/FeC@NHC with tunable N-doped carbon shell thickness is well crafted. The Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 with the best electrocatalytic performance is obtained by optimizing the thickness of N-doped hollow carbon shell. The Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 exhibits the highest activity for the OER and outstanding stability and durability. Hence, this research may establish a promising path for the rational design of metals@carbon composite with controllable structure which can act as highly efficient electrocatalysts for oxygen evolution reaction (OER).
Keywords: Multi-core-shell structure; N-doped hollow carbon; Tunable carbon shell thickness; Oxygen evolution reaction
## 1. Introduction
Promoted by the global energy crisis and environmental pollution, the gradually development of clean and renewable energy resources has attracted great attention from the scientific field [1-3]. Electrocatalytic oxygen evolution reaction (OER, $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\rightarrow\mathrm{O}_{2}+4\mathrm{H}^{+}+4\mathrm{e}^{-})$ as an anodic half reaction in the water splitting plays a vital role in clean and renewable energy [4]. Relative to conventional approaches, OER is a prospective way to renewable energy. But the efficiency of OER is so impeded by sluggish kinetics that needs a larger overpotential to overcome the kinetic barrier due to a multiple electron transfer process [5-7]. Nowadays, iridium and ruthenium oxides ( $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ ) with excellent electrocatalytic performances have been considered as up-to-the-minute catalysts for OER. Frankly speaking, the high price and low abundance limit the large-scale productions and applications of $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ . Nevertheless, unlike the $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , transition metal and transition metal oxides with low cost and high abundance possess an enormous potential as substitutable OER electrocatalysts [8, 9].
To solve the above issues, many researches have been launched to develop highly efficient OER electrocatalysts, including transition metal/transition metal oxides composites [10, 11], chalcogenides [12], metal-free carbons [13, 14] and carbon-based composites [15, 16]. For example, He et al. has developed Ni/Fe3O4 composite that displayed excellent electrocatalytic property for OER [17]. Wu and coworkers have proven that self-supported Ni-Fe-Sn film on Ni foam have high catalytic activity toward OER [18]. Das et al. has reported a nickel/molybdenum carbide-anchored N-doped graphene/CNT hybrid for OER, and the enhanced electrocatalytic performance was attributed to the synergistic effects by nickel/molybdenum carbide and N-doped graphene/CNT in hybrid [19]. Ullah and coworkers have designed M-MO $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni}$ , Co) in 3D graphene composite, which was found to display remarkable OER catalytic performance [20]. Although some works have made good progress for OER catalysts, it is still a challenge to prepare low-cost electrocatalyst with powerful OER performance by facile and mild approach.
Up to now, hollow carbon materials have become highly attractive for OER electrocatalysis due to their excellent properties, such as robust thermal stability, large interior void space fraction chemical inertness and high conductivity [21, 22]. Nevertheless, most OER catalysts are single hollow carbon spheres; their tedious preparation procedure and electrocatalytic performance need to be further improved [14, 23]. Therefore, rational design and preparation of exquisitely multi-core-shell structure composite might be a suitable approach to enhance the electrocatalytic performance and simplify the experimental process. Traditional synthetic approaches for obtaining multi-core-shell composites regular need the use of a hard template, such as SiO2 nanoparticles [24, 25]. Typically, template removal often involves complex and strict treatment, which is undoubtedly uneconomical and time-consuming. In recent years, because of interesting architectures and outstanding properties of MOFs, such as high surface area, high thermal, mechanical stability and open metal sites, MOFs have become a hot spot in nanocatalysts [26-28]. MOFs as promising precursors for preparation of multi-core-shell composites. After high temperature calcination, MOFs can be successfully converted into nanoparticles/carbon hybrid nanomaterials. Meanwhile, hollow carbon shell displays a high electronic conductivity and excellent electrochemical steadiness, it can protect the decomposition of MOFs core. Hollow carbon shell on a metallic/carbon hybrid core has exhibited well promise in the improvement of the stability and durability in OER. However, to the best of our knowledge, there have been little researches on constructing the different carbon shell thickness of multi-core-shell heterostructures via MOF-deriving approaches.
Herein, inspired by the above discussion, we reported and prepared a facile approach for constructing a multiple core-shell structure with MOF-derived Co/FeC nanoparticles as the core and a N-doped hollow carbon as the shell (Co/FeC@NHC), accompanied with the delivery of MOF morphology. A distinctive core-shell structure consisting of a bimetal-based MOF (Co/Fe) core and polydopamine shell was crafted, followed by high-temperature pyrolysis and subsequent hydrochloric acid etching procesyourenses to synthesize multiple Co/FeC nanoparticles encapsulated in N-doped hollow carbon. Impressively, bimetal-based MOF (Co/Fe) with cubic morphology was formed by simple coprecipitation approach at room temperature. Meanwhile, the synergistic interaction between the N-doped hollow carbon and Co/FeC particles may greatly influence the comprehensive performance of the catalyst. PDA is used as a morphology regulator playing a critical role in formation of core-shell structure, as well as a carbon source and nitrogen source decomposing at high temperature and in-situ forming the carbon matrix. Notably, multi-core-shell structure composites have adjustable shell diameters ranging from $4.8~\mathrm{nm}$ to $47.8~\mathrm{nm}$ through simply adjusting the amounts of PDA. Benefiting from distinctive multiple Co/FeC nanoparticles $@$ N-doped hollow carbon core-shell structure, the optimized Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 showed high electrocatalytic activity and long-term stability and durability for OER electrocatalysis. In addition, compared with the spherical core-shell structure catalysts and some square core-shell structure catalysts, the square Co/FeC@NHC catalysts exhibited simple and mild synthesis strategies and excellent electrocatalytic properties, such as low Tafel slope, low overpotential and high stability. All in all, the well-designed Co/FeC $@$ NHC composites show excellent promise in OER electrocatalysis.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of Co/Fe cubic particles
71 mg CoCl2·H2O and 0.5 mL $37\mathrm{wt}\%$ HCl were dispersed in $200~\mathrm{mL}$ DI water (solution A), and $125\ \mathrm{mg\K_{3}F e(C N)_{6}}$ was added into $250~\mathrm{mL}$ DI water (solution B). Then, the mixed solution of the solution A and B was ultrasound for $30\mathrm{min}$ , followed by aged for $24\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature (RT) in the dark environment. Subsequently, the Co/Fe cubic particles were acquired by centrifugation at $9000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $8\mathrm{min}$ and then dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.2 Synthesis of Co/Fe@PDA-X
60 mg Co/Fe cubic particles were dispersed in $120~\mathrm{mL}$ DI water under ultrasonic bath for $10\mathrm{min}$ , and $45.5\mathrm{mg}$ Tris was quickly added into the above suspension $\mathrm{\DeltapH=}$ 8.5). Afterwards, $75~\mathrm{mg}$ dopamine (DA) was slowly added into the mixture under magnetic stirring. The suspension solution was then stirred for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in the dark environment at RT. The obtained product was gathered by centrifugation at $6000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 5 min, washed with DI water three times, and then dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. The resulting product was expressed Co/Fe@PDA-1. In order to control the formation of hollow carbon shells with different thicknesses, similar preparation processes were adopted according to the above procedure by only changing the amounts of Tris and DA. Specifically, the amounts of Tris and DA were adjusted to 91 mg and $150~\mathrm{mg}$ (Co/Fe@PDA-2), $136.5~\mathrm{mg}$ and $225~\mathrm{mg}$ (Co/Fe@PDA-3), $182~\mathrm{mg}$ and 300 mg Co/Fe $@$ PDA-4), $273\mathrm{mg}$ and $450\mathrm{mg}$ (Co/Fe@PDA-5), respectively.
## 2.3 Synthesis of Co/FeC@N doped hollow carbon core shell compounds (Co/FeC@NHC-X)
The above Co/Fe $@$ PDA-X composites were calcinated under nitrogen atmosphere and kept at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1 h. Then, the obtained products were etched in $6\mathbf{M}$ HCl solution for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ at RT. Subsequently, the above dispersion was centrifuged at 9000 rpm for 5 min, the was washed with 6 M HCl and DI water for three times and then dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum overnight, which was denoted as Co/FeC@NHC-X.
## 3. Results and discussion
The preparation of Co/FeC@NHC-X composites is displayed in Scheme 1. In the first step, the uniform Co/Fe particle as the precursor was prepared through a simple and mild coprecipitation. As-prepared Co/Fe particle displays a uniform cubic morphology with a size of 104 nm. In the second step, an oxidative self-polymerization was used to wrap the polydopamine with different amounts on the surface of the Co/Fe cubic particle, leading to the formation of Co/Fe $@$ PDA-X with different PDA shell thicknesses. The DA amount is the vital factor to form tunable carbon shell thickness. Specifically, when increasing the amount of DA, the structure of Co/Fe $@$ PDA-X altered sequentially from the Co/Fe@PDA-1 with thin PDA shell to Co/Fe@PDA-5 with thick PDA shell. In the final step, Co/Fe@PDA-X was calcined at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, and subsequent etched, resulting in the generation of multiple Co/FeC nanoparticles encapsulated in N-doped hollow carbon. Nitrogen-doping can also effectively adjust the electronic structure of the neighboring carbon atoms, so then improve their electrocatalytic properties [29]. The calcination treatment and etching process both play significant roles in the formation of multi-core-shell Co/FeC@N-doped hollow carbon. Notably, the Co/Fe cubic particle was gradually reduced to multiple Co/FeC nanoparticles, and the external wrapped PDA shells with different thickness were carbonized to form a robust N-doped hollow carbon shells under heating and etching procedures, which resulted to formation of the Co/FeC@NHC-X with controllable carbon shell thickness.
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images show more details of the morphology of all composites. As shown in Fig. 1, Co/Fe particle showed a homogeneous cube structure with average particle diameter of ${\sim}104~\mathrm{nm}$ . Fig. 2 shows Co/FeC nanoparticles distributed evenly in the N-doped hollow carbon shell with different shell thickness. The Co/Fe cubic particle serves as precursor template to establish the hollow space while the pyrolysis and acid etching of polydopamine shell leads to forming a robust carbon shell with different thickness to protect the Co/FeC core. Interestingly, Fig. 2A shows a specific morphology of Co/FeC@NHC-1, where the cube’s appearance of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 is retained well after high temperature pyrolysis and acid etching. Numerous Co/FeC nanoparticles are observed inside the N-doped hollow carbon shell with $4.8~\mathrm{nm}$ diameter. Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 has a uniform size and appearance (Fig. 3A), and the high resolution TEM (HRTEM) image shows that the inner Co/FeC core is coated with a carbon shell, in which the carbon shell makes up of well-ordered layers with a lattice spacing of about $0.34~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , corresponding to the (002) planes of graphitic carbon (Fig.2F) [30]. The EDS spectrum of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 was displayed in Fig. 3B, which demonstrated the presence of C, N, O, Co, Fe elements of Co/FeC@NHC-1. In addition, Elemental mapping images revealed that C, N, O, Fe and Co elements were highly distributed throughout the entire core shell architecture (Fig. 4), confirming the existence of C, N, O, Fe and Co elements in Co/FeC@NHC-1. It's remarkable that many N elements can be detected in the Co/FeC@NHC-1, which is beneficial to enhancing the electrochemical performance [31]. Besides, the TEM images show the morphology of Co/FeC@NHC-2 (Fig. 2B), Co/FeC@NHC-3 (Fig. 2C), Co/FeC@NHC-4 (Fig. 2D) and Co/FeC@NHC-5 (Fig. 2E). It can be distinctly observed that as the dopamine content increases, the thickness of N-doped hollow carbon shell of these four composites also increases, and the thickness are $5.3\mathrm{nm}$ , $7.5\mathrm{nm}$ , $12.2\mathrm{nm}$ and $47.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. With the increase of the amount of DA, the shell edge of Co/FeC@NHC-X becomes blurred. In addition, for comparison, ${\mathrm{Cu/Fe}}$ particles and Cu/FeC@NHC were synthesized by the same approach (Supporting information). Fig. S1 distinctly reveals that the Cu/Fe particles possess a non-uniform sized cubic structure. The TEM images of Cu/FeC@NHC display circular carbon shell (Fig. S2), Cu/Fe particles are gradually reduced to multiple Cu/FeC nanoparticles, and the external wrapped PDA layer is carbonized to form a carbon shell with blurred edge.
Compared with Co/FeC@NHC-1, Cu/FeC $@$ NHC has no obvious carbon shell and uniform morphology, which may be due to the ${\mathrm{Cu/Fe}}$ particle precursor has no regular and uniform cubic configuration.
As shown in Fig. 5, the X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern was used to explore the structure of the Co/FeC@NHC-1, Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4 and Co/FeC@NHC-5 composites, and the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectrum was utilized to investigate the elemental composition and chemical state of the Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 composite. As shown in Fig. 5A, the XRD patterns of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-(1-5) composites exhibit almost the same and major diffraction peaks, which are obtained at $2\theta{=}44.93^{\circ}$ , $50.52^{\circ}$ , $69.64^{\circ}$ , $82.44^{\circ}$ , according to the XRD standard spectrum of FeC (JCPDS No. 06-0686). In addition, the five composites all reveal distinct diffraction peaks that can be indexed to Co (JCPDS No. 05-0727), which are obtained at $2\theta{=}44.76^{\circ}$ , $47.57^{\circ}$ , $62.73^{\circ}$ , $75.94^{\circ}$ and the above diffraction peaks assigned to the (002), (101), (102) and (110) reflection of Co, respectively. According the above discussion, indicating FeC and Co are existed in the Co/FeC@NHC-(1-5) composites. In addition, the XRD patterns of Co/FeC@NHC-(1-5) display a wide diffraction peak about $25^{\circ}$ , which can be attributed to the (002) reflection of graphitic carbon [32, 33], indicating that carbon species is formed in the preparation approach. Hence, the XRD results demonstrate that the composites are consisted of graphite carbon, Co and FeC. In addition, as shown in Fig. S3, XRD pattern of the Cu/FeC@NHC suggested the characteristic peaks of FeC (JCPDS No. 06-0686) and Cu (JCPDS No. 85-1326) in the
Cu/FeC@NHC, indicating FeC and Cu are existed in the Cu/FeC@NHC composite. As presented in Fig. 5B, the survey XPS spectrum of Co/FeC@NHC-1 shows five elements of C, N, O, Fe and Co. Meanwhile, the high resolution spectrum of C 1s, N 1s, Co 2p and Fe 2p further prove the existence of C, N, Fe and Co in the Co/FeC@NHC-1. The N 1s XPS spectrum of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1can be fitted into three peaks (Fig. 5C); the main peak at $400.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ is belonged to pyrrolic N, whereas the other two weak peaks at $398.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $401.3{\mathrm{~eV}}$ can be allocated to pyridinic N and graphitic N, respectively, suggesting abundant N atoms are introduced into the carbon-carbon bonds by the synthesis strategy of Co/FeC@NHC-1. The Fig. 5E diagram is the peaks of $\mathrm{Co}\ 2\mathrm{p}$ for the Co/FeC@NHC-1, and the peaks of $\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}3/2$ at $785.9\mathrm{eV}$ and $802.6\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $\mathrm{Co_{x}O_{y}}$ species [34], while the peak of Co 2p1/2 at 780.6 and $796.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be attributed to ${\bf C o}^{0}$ [35, 36]. In addition, in Fig. 5F, the two peaks at 709.9 and $723.9{\mathrm{~eV}}$ are ascribed to Fe 2p3/2 and Fe 2p1/2, respectively. From the above diagrams of Fig. 5 we can come to the conclusion that the C, N, O, Fe and Co were successfully existed in Co/FeC@NHC-1.
The electrocatalytic performances of the Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ for the OER were investigated in 1.0 M KOH solution. The above composites were examined using linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) at a scan rate of $2\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ , and LSV curves of Co/FeC@NHC-1, Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 and RuO2 were as shown in Fig. 6A. Except for RuO2, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 shows the lowest overpotential of $380~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , indicating the N-doped hollow carbon with proper shell thickness is beneficial to the exchange of substances. Conversely, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ need overpotentials of 420, 450, 470, 480 and $350\mathrm{mV},$ respectively. This small overpotential value of the Co/FeC@NHC-1confirms that the Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 has better OER activity than Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-3, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4 and Co/FeC@NHC-5. To further research the electrocatalytic kinetics of the OER, Tafel slopes are shown in Fig. 6B. The excellent OER electrocatalytic performance of Co/FeC@NHC-1 is also evident by its low Tafel slope of ${70\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}}}$ , indicating more favorable reaction kinetics. On the contrary, the Co/FeC $@$ NHC-2, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-3, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 and ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ have a Tafel slope of $80~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1},119~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}$ , 122 $\mathrm{{mV}~d e c^{-1}}$ , $125~\mathrm{\mV}$ dec−1 and $79~\mathrm{\mV~\dec^{-1}}$ , respectively. The Tafel slope of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 is significantly lower than commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ . Obviously, the Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 possesses higher OER activity, shows smaller overpotential and Tafel slope than Co/FeC@NHC-(2-5). Impressively, the Tafel slope increases with the decreased carbon shell-thickness, which shows that the thinner shell is benefit to the electrocatalytic performance [37]. Thus, the reasonable design of appropriate carbon shell thickness can effectively enhance the electrochemical properties of Co/FeC@NHC-X composites [38]. In the Co/FeC@NHC-X core shell structure, the N-doped carbon-based shell contributes to good electron transfer, and it acts as a synergistic role with the embedded Co/FeC nanoparticles, resulting to an outstanding property in catalyzing OER. To be specific, due to the unique advantages of N-doped carbon materials, it is an ideal non noble metal monatomic nanomaterials. It is a kind of excellent electrocatalyst in itself. The electronic effect caused by strong electronegativity $\mathbf{N}$ and C provides more anchor sites for non noble metals, and the intrinsic defects of nitrogen-doped and carbon materials provide more stable additional attachment sites for metal atoms [39,40]. In addition, Co/FeC core with bimetallic active site has many advantages, such as high stability, conductivity and low price [41-43]. Meanwhile, the hierarchically porous nanostructure on N-doped hollow carbon could not only expose more active sites for OER but also markedly accelerate the transport of mass and charge within electrodes. The outside N-doped hollow carbon shell of the encapsulated Co/FeC core could protect the inside Co/FeC core from the corrosion of strongly alkaline electrolyte to ensure outstanding stability for them [44]
To gain further insight into the catalytic activity of Co/FeC@NHC-1, Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC@NHC-4 and Co/FeC@NHC-5, electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) measurement was adopted by employing the solution of $5\mathrm{mMFe(CN)}_{6}\mathrm{\Omega}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}^{3-/4-}$ and 0.1 M KCl. Fig. S3F shows the Nyquist impedance spectra of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1/GCE, Co/FeC@NHC-2/GCE, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-3/GCE, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4/GCE, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5/GCE and bare GCE. The inset of Fig. S3F is equivalent circuit that containing the solution resistance $(R_{s})$ , the double layer capacitance $(C_{d l})$ , the Warburg impedance $(Z\omega)$ , and the electron transfer resistance $(R_{c t})$ . The bare GCE consists of a semicircular and a linear part, which means that the higher frequency semicircular part is equivalent to the electron transfer limited process [45]. The $R_{c t}$ value for bare GCE is calculated as $227\Omega$ by using ZsimpWin software. Obviously, the semicircle was transformed to a straight line of Co/FeC@NHC-(1-5)/GCE, demonstrating that the Co/FeC@NHC-(1-5) have a pretty small impedance and well electrical conductivity. The above results reveal that the Co/FeC nanoparticles encapsulated in N-doped hollow carbon core-shell structure can enhance electron transport property at the electrolyte–electrode interface, which will expedite the electrocatalytic procedure.
Cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were performed to explore the electrochemical properties of the Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1, Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC@NHC-4 and Co/FeC@NHC-5 by using a standard three-electrode system. The double-layer capacitance $(C_{d I})$ of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1, Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4 and Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 were measured by employing the solution of 1 M KOH electrolyte [46]. Clearly, Fig. 7A-E show the current density increases with the increased scan rates. As shown in Fig. 7A-E, therefore, the CdI values of the Co/FeC@NHC-1, Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-3, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4 and Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 are calculated by using the equation $\Delta j=j_{a}-j_{c}$ at $+0.26\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE against the CV scan rates [47]. Consequently, The $C_{d I}$ of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1, Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC@NHC-4 and Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 are 61.44, 59.66, 24.56, 24.54 and $23.27~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ , respectively. As we all know, the electrocatalytic active surface areas (ECSA) are calculated by the $C_{d I}$ , and the CdI can be measured by CV methods. The ECSA of the all catalysts were evaluated (Methods in the Supporting Information). Compared with other composites, it can be seen from Table S2 that Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 has the largest value of roughness and ECSA, indicating that the Co/FeC@NHC-1 provides more larger active surface area for OER reaction.
For OER activity and kinetics, the stability and durability are also key factors for assessing their electrochemical performance. Meanwhile, LSV after 1000 continuous CV cycles and chronopotentiometry test were also conducted to assess their stability and durability. As shown in Fig. 8A, the LSV curves of Co/FeC@NHC-1, Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC@NHC-4 and Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 after CV of 1000 cycles are almost overlapped with the original one, speak specifically, the current loss of Co/FeC@NHC-1, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-2, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-3, Co/FeC@NHC-4 and Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 are just $0.1\%$ , $0.2\%$ , $0.22\%$ , $0.3\%$ and $0.8\%$ , respectively. As shown in Fig. 8B, the chronopotentiometry test indicates that at a constant potential of 1.54 vs. RHE, the overall stability of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1 is steady with extremely small current loss of $8.89\%$ over the $11\mathrm{~h~}$ period. These results clarify that Co/FeC $@$ NHC-X composites have outstanding OER durability and stability, which is vital for practical applications.
In addition, the proton transport process of the Co/FeC@NHC-1, Co/FeC@NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-4 and Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 were measured by using LSV at different scan rates $\mathrm{\Omega(2{-}60\ m V s^{-1}},$ ). As shown as Fig. S3, at different scan rates, the current density ratio of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-1, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-2, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-3, Co/FeC@NHC-4 and Co/FeC $@$ NHC-5 are $105.1\%$ , $117.2\%$ , $140.9\%$ , $144.7\%$ and $153.3\%$ , respectively. Compared to Co/FeC $@$ NHC-2, Co/FeC@NHC-3, Co/FeC@NHC-4 and Co/FeC@NHC-5, each LSV curve of Co/FeC@NHC-1 is very close at different scan rates. Thus, Co/FeC@NHC-1 has very small current increase, indicating Co/FeC@NHC-1 has the efficient transmission capability of charges and reactant.
## Conclusion
In summary, an uncomplicated and economic strategy is proposed for preparing multi-core-shell structure Co/FeC nanoparticles encapsulated in N-doped hollow carbon (Co/FeC $@$ NHC) with different shell thicknesses. bimetal-based MOF (Co/Fe) cubic particle serves as core was fabricated by a mild way at room temperature, and N-doped hollow carbon More importantly, the multiple Co/FeC cores are embedded in a N-doped carbon shell with an adequate space. In this unique multi-core-shell structure, the N-doped hollow carbon shell can powerfully bear the volume variation and protect the Co/FeC core in the OER process. In addition, in order to optimize the electrocatalytic performance of Co/FeC $@$ NHC-X with different carbon shell thicknesses, Co/FeC $@$ NHC-X with controllable carbon shell thickness can be crafted by coating various contents of PDA on Co/Fe cubic particle precursor. Impressively, compared with Co/FeC $@$ NHC-(2-5), Co/FeC@NHC-1 with the thinnest N-doped carbon shell displayed better electrocatalytic performance, including small Tafel slope of $70\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , low overpotential of $380\mathrm{mV}$ and large double-layer capacitance of $61.44~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ . Additionally, the robust construction of N-doped carbon shell could keep superior stability and durability for OER. Compared with previous works, the preparation strategy of the novel multi-core-shell structure Co/FeC@NHC is simpler and gentler and has more excellent OER performance. As stated above, this research may establish a promising strategy for the reasonable design of multi-core-shell composite with controllable structure for OER electrocatalysts.
